---
/**
 * CalloutListBlock — the `callout_list` layout: a centred section head over a
 * single column of sage-circle bullets, and nothing else.
 *
 * EXTRACTED, NOT DESIGNED. The markup is the `#emergencies` band of
 * src/pages/emergency-dentistry/index.astro:329-343 — `section.section` >
 * `.wrap` > `.section-head` > `ul` of `li` — element for element. The rules in
 * <style> are src/styles/pages/emergency-dentistry.css:713-745 plus that
 * sheet's band shell, with the `.emerg` namespace stripped and its local
 * `--sage` / `--ink` / `--ink-soft` / `--f-body` aliases resolved back to the
 * `--vs-*` tokens they alias one-for-one (emergency-dentistry.css:41-63).
 * Rendered values are unchanged.
 *
 * ── THE ONE CLASS RENAME, STATED SO THE DIFF DOES NOT HAVE TO FIND IT ──────
 * The template writes `<ul class="emerg-callout-list">`; this block writes
 * `<ul class="callout-list">`. That is a real, deliberate one-token change to
 * the built HTML of /emergency-dentistry/ and it belongs in the block-map
 * alongside ProcessStepsBlock's `.process-foot` → `.process-outro`.
 *
 * It is not a preference. The page sheet's own header calls this pattern a
 * "direct lift of .priv-callout-list from privacy-policy.css with namespace
 * swap" (emergency-dentistry.css:26-28), so the same shape ships under TWO
 * page-local names — `.emerg-callout-list` and `.priv-callout-list`. A shared
 * block emits one class, so one of the two names has to go whatever we choose,
 * and the house rule for that choice is already settled: FaqBlock emits
 * `.faq-list` where the page sheet says `.caligners .faq-list`. Stripping the
 * namespace here means stripping it from BOTH the ancestor and the element
 * name, because on this page the namespace is baked into both.
 *
 * The rename also removes, rather than wins, the specificity fight the sheet
 * would otherwise pick: `.emerg .emerg-callout-list` is (0,2,0) and Astro
 * compiles a single scoped compound to `.callout-list[cid]`, also (0,2,0) —
 * a tie decided by bundle order, which is the one thing this project has
 * agreed never to build on. Belt and braces: every rule below that touches the
 * list is written `.section .callout-list …` → (0,4,0) at minimum, the same
 * idiom and for the same reason as CandidacyLedgerBlock's `.section .cand-grid`
 * (CandidacyLedgerBlock.astro:172-182). Even if a page sheet later grows a
 * `.emerg .callout-list` rule, this block still wins outright.
 *
 * The shell primitives (`.wrap`, `.eyebrow`, `h2`, `p`, `.section-head`) are
 * left unqualified, as in every sibling block: those DO tie with the page
 * sheet, and it is harmless because both sides declare the same values.
 *
 * ── WHY `item` IS `set:html` ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 * Because the seven live lines carry markup and always have: every one opens
 * `<b>Severe toothache:</b>`, and line three links mid-sentence to
 * /cosmetic-dentistry/ through `<a class="vs-link">`
 * (emergency-dentistry/index.astro:126-132). The template already renders them
 * `<li set:html={c}>`; escaping them here would print the tags at the patient.
 * The trust boundary is the practice's own editors, not public input.
 *
 * ── WHY `lead` EXISTS AND DRAWS NOTHING TODAY ─────────────────────────────
 * `lines` comes from the shared block_list_field() factory
 * (vs-content-model.php:462-491), so `lead` — the optional bold opening —
 * arrives with it. It is BLANK on all seven live rows, because on this page the
 * `<b>` and its colon live inside `item`, which is where the template has
 * always had them; splitting them out would move a colon and a space. Blank
 * therefore has to render byte-for-byte nothing: no `<b>`, no separator. The
 * filled branch follows the house shape used by CardGridBlock.astro:422,
 * ComparisonCardsBlock.astro:550 and MediaSplitBlock.astro:773 — `<b>lead</b>`,
 * one space, then the line.
 *
 * That one space is written `{" "}` and not as a literal space in the source.
 * MediaSplitBlock.astro:807-817 records the measurement: the compiler collapses
 * a bare space between an expression and an element on the same line, and here
 * the right-hand side is a `<Fragment set:html>` rather than an expression, so
 * the "element-then-expression keeps its space" escape does not apply. An
 * explicit `{" "}` is the form that survives — it is what rescued
 * `300+ <span class="stars">` on sinus-lift.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE THROWS — an editor triggers deploys and never sees the output
 * (cms/mu-plugins/vs-deploy.php:142-158). Every field is read as if it might be
 * missing, blank, or the wrong type.
 *
 * NO CTA FIELDS. Neither band this layout serves draws a button row, and the
 * manifest deliberately selects none — a field nothing renders reads as
 * supported, which is this project's single most repeated failure.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 1.2, 2.5, 3.3.
 */

/** One row of the layout's `lines` repeater (PageFieldsBlocksLines). */
export interface CalloutLine {
  /** The optional bold opening, including whatever punctuation ends it. Plain
   *  text — printed escaped, unlike `item`. Blank on every live row. */
  lead?: string | null;
  /** The line itself. CARRIES MARKUP — `<b>`, and one inline `<a>`. */
  item?: string | null;
}

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. Absent when blank. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  /** The paragraph under the heading, inside `.section-head`. */
  body?: string | null;
  lines?: (CalloutLine | null)[] | null;
}

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class the page's markup already uses.
 *
 * Read this map, not an ancestor class. `.section.alt` is charcoal green on
 * emergency-dentistry.css:327 and cream on teeth-whitening.css:237 (Fact 4), so
 * a block that inherited its band would be wrong on some page the moment a
 * section is dragged. `paper` maps to nothing because a paper band is a bare
 * `class="section"` in every template — and `#emergencies` is a paper band, so
 * emitting `class="section paper"` would be the diff.
 */
const BAND_CLASS: Record<string, string | undefined> = {
  paper: undefined,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

const {
  /* `paper` and not `sage-pale`: it is what the PHP field stores as its own
     default_value (vs-content-model.php, block_preamble()), so a row an editor
     has never opened renders the band wp-admin is showing them. */
  id,
  band = "paper",
  eyebrow,
  heading,
  body,
  lines,
} = Astro.props;

const bandKey = typeof band === "string" && band !== "" ? band : "paper";
const bandClass = BAND_CLASS[bandKey];

/** Trim once, here, so every guard below tests for real content rather than for
 *  a field an editor left as a single space. */
const text = (input: unknown): string =>
  typeof input === "string" ? input.trim() : "";

/**
 * The eyebrow carries `light` on the two bands the templates spell
 * `.section.dark` / `.section.sage`. INERT under the current cascade
 * (`.section.dark .eyebrow` at (0,3,0) already beats `.eyebrow.light` at
 * (0,2,0)); reproduced because this phase is verified by an HTML diff, not by a
 * rendering. Same rule and wording as CandidacyLedgerBlock.astro:236-244.
 *
 * The live band is paper, so this is false there and the emitted span is the
 * template's bare `<span class="eyebrow">`.
 */
const eyebrowLight = bandKey === "sage-pale" || bandKey === "sage";

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);
const hasHead = eyebrowText !== "" || headingHtml !== "" || bodyText !== "";

/**
 * A row with nothing in EITHER sub-field is a line the editor added and
 * abandoned — ACF leaves one behind whenever someone clicks "Add a line" and
 * changes their mind. Unfiltered it draws a bare sage circle with no text
 * beside it, which reads as a rendering bug rather than as an empty field.
 * Same filter, same reason, as MediaSplitBlock.astro:633-637.
 */
const lineRows = (Array.isArray(lines) ? lines : [])
  .map((row) => ({ lead: text(row?.lead), item: text(row?.item) }))
  .filter((row) => row.lead !== "" || row.item !== "");

/*
 * THREE FORMATTING RULES THE TEMPLATE BELOW DEPENDS ON. All three were measured
 * by rendering this block beside the band it replaces, not reasoned about:
 * before they were applied the two outputs differed in three places, and after
 * them they differ in exactly one — the deliberate class rename in the header.
 *
 * 1. The `<li>`'s two branches sit on ONE template line. Breaking the
 *    `set:html` Fragment onto its own line puts a newline-and-indent inside the
 *    `<li>`, which is a leading space on the plain-line branch — the branch all
 *    seven live rows take. Same constraint MediaSplitBlock.astro:625-631
 *    records for its checklist.
 *
 * 2. The `<h2>` is written `<h2><Fragment set:html={…} /></h2>` on one line,
 *    the shape StatCalloutBlock.astro uses and NOT the indented shape FaqBlock
 *    and CandidacyLedgerBlock use. The indented one emits `<h2> Heading </h2>`
 *    — a space either side — where every template in this family writes
 *    `<h2>Heading</h2>`. Both render identically; only one diffs clean.
 *
 * 3. There are no JSX comments anywhere in the template. A comment on its own
 *    line inside an element leaves a SECOND whitespace-only text node beside
 *    it: the note that used to sit above this `<h2>` shipped `</span>  <h2>`
 *    with two spaces, and the note that used to sit inside the `<ul>` shipped
 *    a blank line before the first `<li>`. Notes about the markup live here in
 *    the frontmatter, where they emit nothing at all.
 */

/**
 * No lines and no head copy is an untouched row, and it must draw NOTHING
 * extra — not an empty `<ul>`, not a bare band of padding. The `<ul>` is
 * suppressed independently of the head so an unfinished band still shows the
 * heading its editor has written.
 */
const sectionId = text(id) || undefined;
---

<section class:list={["section", bandClass]} id={sectionId} data-vs-reveal-batch>
  <div class="wrap">
    {
      hasHead && (
        <div class="section-head">
          {eyebrowText && (
            <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrowText}</span>
          )}
          {headingHtml && <h2><Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /></h2>}
          {bodyText && <p>{bodyText}</p>}
        </div>
      )
    }

    {
      lineRows.length > 0 && (
        <ul class="callout-list">
          {lineRows.map((line) => (
            <li>{line.lead === "" ? <Fragment set:html={line.item} /> : <Fragment><b>{line.lead}</b>{" "}<Fragment set:html={line.item} /></Fragment>}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )
    }
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ══ WHERE THESE RULES COME FROM ═══════════════════════════════════════════
     src/styles/pages/emergency-dentistry.css, `.emerg`, with the namespace
     stripped and the sheet's local aliases resolved to the `--vs-*` tokens they
     alias one-for-one (emergency-dentistry.css:41-63):
       --sage → --vs-sage · --sage-soft → --vs-sage-soft · --ink → --vs-ink
       --ink-soft → --vs-ink-soft · --f-body → --vs-font-body
       --f-display → --vs-font-display
     Rendered values are unchanged.

     Repeated per block rather than shared: a shared sheet is an ancestor by
     another name, and the property that makes a block safe to drop on any page
     is that nothing outside it decides how it looks (R3). */

  /* ---------- band shell ----------
     `.section` owns its padding and has no margin at all, so this band cannot
     collapse into whichever band an editor drags above or below it. Same rule
     as FinalBand.astro and every sibling block.
     padding: emergency-dentistry.css:324-326.
     `cream` and `sage` are not in this sheet — the page places neither band —
     so their values come from tokens.css via the block family's shell
     (FaqBlock.astro), which is the contract the band select promises. */
  .section {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 110px 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.cream {
    background: var(--vs-cream);
  }
  /* emergency-dentistry.css:350-352 */
  .section.dark {
    background: var(--vs-sage-pale);
  }
  .section.sage {
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  /* emergency-dentistry.css:327-330 */
  .section.alt {
    background: var(--vs-charcoal-green);
  }

  /* Keep the heading clear of the stacked global Nav + sticky TOC when a rail
     chip is clicked — and /emergency-dentistry/ has a rail on all eight bands.
     emergency-dentistry.css:318-320 (written there as `.emerg section[id]`).
     Both custom properties are set on the page wrapper and inherit down; the
     fallbacks are the desktop measurements that sheet declares
     (emergency-dentistry.css:42-43). */
  .section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h, 77px) + var(--toc-h, 68px) + 12px);
  }

  /* emergency-dentistry.css:65-69 */
  .wrap {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  /* emergency-dentistry.css:71-78 */
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  /* emergency-dentistry.css:79-81 */
  .eyebrow.light {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }

  /* h2 resolves to ink. The page sheet reaches that colour only through a
     trailing override whose own comment says it works because of its file
     position (emergency-dentistry.css:1283-1289); a block that owns its cascade
     states the outcome once instead of inheriting a source-order argument it
     can no longer see. Sizing: emergency-dentistry.css:104-108. */
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.2vw, 54px);
  }
  /* `:global()` because the `<em>` arrives through `set:html` and carries no
     scope attribute — a plain `h2 em` compiles to `em[cid]`, misses it, and
     drops the accent colour. Value: emergency-dentistry.css:124-128. */
  h2 :global(em) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    font-weight: 300;
  }

  /* emergency-dentistry.css:120-123 */
  p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* emergency-dentistry.css:385-416 */
  .section-head {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0 auto 64px;
    text-align: center;
  }
  .section-head .eyebrow {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    content: "";
    width: 28px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section-head h2 {
    margin: 22px 0 22px;
  }
  .section-head p {
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  /* ---------- band inversions ----------
     Scoped to `.section-head`, not written band-wide as the page sheet writes
     them (emergency-dentistry.css:331-371). The list is body copy on the band's
     own ground, so a band-wide `.section.alt p { … }` is harmless here, but the
     list's own colours are stated against each band below rather than inherited
     — keeping the inversion narrow is what lets that stay true.
     Values: emergency-dentistry.css:331-349 (alt) and :353-371 (dark). */
  .section.alt .section-head h2,
  .section.sage .section-head h2 {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt .section-head h2 :global(em),
  .section.sage .section-head h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head p,
  .section.sage .section-head p {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.alt .section-head .eyebrow::after,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.sage .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
  }

  .section.dark .section-head h2 {
    color: var(--vs-ink);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-sage);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head p,
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow::before,
  .section.dark .section-head .eyebrow::after {
    background: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* ---------- the list ----------
     emergency-dentistry.css:713-745, verbatim but for the namespace and the
     alias resolution. Sage circle bullets, one column, 720px centred.

     EVERY SELECTOR HERE IS AT LEAST TWO COMPOUNDS DEEP — `.section .callout-list`
     compiles to `.section[cid] .callout-list[cid]` at (0,4,0). A single compound
     would be (0,2,0), which is exactly what `.emerg .emerg-callout-list` scores,
     and bundle order would decide. The class rename above already means that
     rule cannot match this markup, so this is the second lock rather than the
     first — see the header. */
  .section .callout-list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: 720px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  .section .callout-list li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 32px;
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }
  .section .callout-list li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.6em;
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border: 2px solid var(--vs-sage);
    background: transparent;
  }
  /* `:global()` because the `<b>` on all seven live rows arrives inside `item`
     through `set:html` and carries no scope attribute — a plain `li b` compiles
     to `b[cid]` and would silently miss every one of them, dropping the bold
     lead-in on the whole list. The authored `<b>` of the `lead` branch is
     matched by the same rule, so both spellings land on one declaration.
     emergency-dentistry.css:741-744. */
  .section .callout-list li :global(b) {
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* The list's copy on the two dark bands.
     Not in the page sheet, because /emergency-dentistry/ places this band on
     paper and no live row is on charcoal or sage. Stated anyway for the reason
     MediaSplitBlock.astro:1202-1212 records at length: the page sheets' own
     dark-band text rules are namespaced under a PAGE class, and a band rendered
     from the CMS has no page class to match — so a callout list an editor drags
     onto charcoal would keep `--vs-ink-soft` and print near-black on charcoal.
     Additive: no saved row is on either band, so nothing that renders today
     moves. */
  .section.alt .callout-list li,
  .section.sage .callout-list li {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.85);
  }
  .section.alt .callout-list li :global(b),
  .section.sage .callout-list li :global(b) {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt .callout-list li::before,
  .section.sage .callout-list li::before {
    border-color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }

  /* The inline `<a class="vs-link">` line three carries, on the two dark bands.
     global.css DOES invert `.vs-link` on charcoal, but it reaches it through a
     hand-maintained list of PAGE-SCOPE classes, which a CMS-rendered band can
     never join — the same trap, and the same answer, as
     MediaSplitBlock.astro:1213-1224. `:global()` because the anchor arrives
     through `set:html` and carries no scope attribute. */
  .section.alt .callout-list :global(.vs-link),
  .section.sage .callout-list :global(.vs-link) {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
    text-decoration-color: rgba(232, 234, 216, 0.5);
  }
  .section.alt .callout-list :global(.vs-link:hover),
  .section.alt .callout-list :global(.vs-link:focus-visible),
  .section.sage .callout-list :global(.vs-link:hover),
  .section.sage .callout-list :global(.vs-link:focus-visible) {
    color: #fff;
    text-decoration-color: #fff;
  }

  /* ---------- responsive ----------
     Re-united with the rules they modify rather than left in a trailing
     "responsive" section 500 lines away (3.2, step 3). The page sheet has one
     breakpoint for this band and it is already the block set's own 780px:
     emergency-dentistry.css:1199 (`.emerg .section`, `.emerg .wrap`,
     `.emerg .section-head`) and :1237-1239 (the list's own 4px gutter). */
  @media (max-width: 780px) {
    .section {
      padding: 70px 0;
    }
    .wrap {
      padding: 0 22px;
    }
    .section-head {
      margin-bottom: 44px;
    }
    .section .callout-list {
      padding: 0 4px;
    }
  }
</style>
